1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,259
When a crisis hits, do the rich run away? When an economy collapses, it triggers

2
00:00:05,259 --> 00:00:07,339
a real offshore capital flight.

3
00:00:07,339 --> 00:00:12,099
We usually think of tax havens as a simple trick to cut taxes,

4
00:00:12,099 --> 00:00:16,820
but take a rich entrepreneur in an emerging nation that has just been hit by a debt

5
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:17,820
crisis.

6
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:21,300
At the start of the emergency, the flight doesn't happen right away.

7
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:26,260
Big capitalists wait, to understand how serious the collapse will become.

8
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:30,700
We might think the goal is to escape an imminent tax hike, and yet,

9
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:35,660
during these collapses, the tax burden on capital often stays stable

10
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:36,660
or even falls.

11
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:39,060
The real terror is expropriation.

12
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:44,380
In fragile systems marked by corruption, elites know that a desperate state

13
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:47,900
could directly confiscate their assets to survive.

14
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:50,420
And this is when the exodus begins.

15
00:00:50,539 --> 00:00:55,940
Around the third year of the crisis, almost a third of bank deposits is diverted

16
00:00:55,940 --> 00:01:00,260
in great secrecy to sealed, inaccessible jurisdictions.

17
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:05,379
This silent hemorrhage drains billions of local wealth, taking away resources

18
00:01:05,379 --> 00:01:10,500
that are vital exactly when they are most needed to restart the country. And what

19
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:12,180
happened to that rich entrepreneur?

20
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:18,180
His funds rest safely abroad, while the citizens who stayed at home pay

21
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,420
the full bill for the collapse.
